  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge is currently sitting above the Plains and will \nslowly inch eastward as an upper level trough digs into the western \nUnited States. Towards the surface, lee troughing will be the main \nfeature across eastern Colorado leading to south to southwest winds \nacross western Kansas through at least Tuesday morning. Skies will \nbe mostly clear with occasional increase in high level cloudiness. \nHighs during this time frame will soar into the upper 80s to low 90s \nwhich is above normal for this time of the year. Lows will generally \nrange from the upper 50s to low 70s. \n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough will then move closer to the \narea during the mid part of next week ejecting disturbances towards \nthe Central High Plains. Precipitation chances increase Tuesday \nafternoon and continue through the mid part of next week. There may \nbe a few strong to severe storms Tuesday afternoon/evening but will \nneed to be monitored through future model runs. Currently SPC has a \nslight risk of sever storms. We then start to dry out by late next \nweek as upper level riding builds across the western United States. \nAs for temperatures, highs in the 70s are expected Wednesday and \nThursday with 60s late week into the weekend. Lows will settle into \nthe 40s by next weekend.",
